🔷 What is Spring Cloud Gateway?
Spring Cloud Gateway is a modern API gateway built on Spring Boot and WebFlux (reactive programming).

📌 Purpose:
It acts as a reverse proxy:

Receives requests from clients

Routes them to the appropriate microservice

Adds features like security, logging, rate-limiting, load balancing, and path rewriting

🧠 In Simple Terms:
It’s the single entry point to all your backend microservices.

🔁 Real-Life Example
Like a receptionist in an office:

You go to the reception desk (Gateway) and say:
"I want to speak to the Sales department (user-service)"

The receptionist looks it up and forwards your request to the right department (microservice)

🧱 What Happens Internally?
Let’s break down internal flow in 7 simple steps:
✅ 1. Client Sends Request to Gateway
📍Example:

GET http://localhost:8080/users/list

✅ 2. Gateway Matches the Route
Based on this config in application.yml:

routes:
  - id: user-service
    uri: lb://user-service
    predicates:
      - Path=/users/**
➡ Gateway checks the incoming request path (/users/list)
➡ It matches the /users/** route.

✅ 3. Eureka Lookup
Since URI is lb://user-service, Gateway knows:

“I need to find a real instance of user-service using Eureka.”

➡ Gateway uses DiscoveryClient to ask Eureka for all user-service instances:

List<ServiceInstance> instances = discoveryClient.getInstances("user-service");
Eureka replies:

user-service is available at:
- http://10.0.0.5:8081
- http://10.0.0.6:8081

✅ 4. Load Balancer Picks One Instance
Spring LoadBalancer picks one instance (e.g., using Round Robin or Random):

ServiceInstance chosen = loadBalancer.choose("user-service");
➡ Chooses: http://10.0.0.5:8081

✅ 5. Gateway Applies Filters (Optional)
Filters can:

Add headers

Rewrite paths

Do logging or authentication

Block requests, etc.

📍Example filter:


filters:
  - AddRequestHeader=X-User-Id, 123

✅ 6. Forward the Request
Gateway forwards the original request to the selected instance:

GET http://10.0.0.5:8081/users/list

✅ 7. Microservice Responds → Gateway Sends Back
➡ The user-service processes the request and sends a response (like user data)

➡ Gateway receives the response and returns it to the original client

🔄 Diagram Overview (Text Form)

Client --> [Gateway] --> [Eureka → get user-service] --> [Pick instance] --> [Forward to Microservice]
                                                             ↑
                                                    Load Balancer
🧰 Internally Used Components
| Component                 | Role                                             |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| **RoutePredicateFactory** | Matches routes (like `/users/**`)                |
| **DiscoveryClient**       | Talks to Eureka                                  |
| **ReactorLoadBalancer**   | Chooses a service instance                       |
| **Netty HTTP Client**     | Makes actual HTTP call                           |
| **Filters**               | Modify request/response (logging, headers, etc.) |


✨ Key Features of Spring Cloud Gateway
✅ Service Discovery (with Eureka or Consul)

✅ Load Balancing (lb://service-name)

✅ Path rewriting

✅ Rate limiting

✅ Security & Auth filters

✅ Circuit Breakers (with Resilience4J)

✅ Reactive (non-blocking) and fast

✅ Summary (One-liner)
Spring Cloud Gateway is a smart, dynamic router that receives client requests, finds the right service using Eureka,
and forwards the request — applying filters, load balancing, and more.



🔷 Step-by-Step: Connect Gateway to Eureka
🔹 1. Add Dependencies to Gateway pom.xml

<dependencies>
    <!-- Spring Cloud Gateway -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-gateway</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Eureka Discovery Client -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-netflix-eureka-client</artifactId>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>

Also include Spring Cloud BOM in <dependencyManagement> if you haven't:

<dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-cloud-dependencies</artifactId>
            <version>2023.0.1</version> <!-- Replace with compatible version -->
            <type>pom</type>
            <scope>import</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</dependencyManagement>


🔹 2. Application Configuration (application.yml)

spring:
  application:
    name: api-gateway

  cloud:
    gateway:
      discovery:
        locator:
          enabled: true   # This allows routing by service name
          lower-case-service-id: true
      routes:
        - id: user-service
          uri: lb://user-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/users/**

        - id: order-service
          uri: lb://order-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/orders/**

eureka:
  client:
    service-url:
      defaultZone: http://localhost:8761/eureka
📝 lb://user-service tells Gateway:

“Use Load Balancer (lb) to find the actual instance of user-service via Eureka.”

🔹 3. Add Eureka Client Annotation in Gateway Main Class

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableEurekaClient
public class ApiGatewayApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(ApiGatewayApplication.class, args);
    }
}
🔹 4. Register All Services with Eureka
Make sure:

Your user-service, order-service, etc. all have:


spring.application.name: user-service
eureka.client.service-url.defaultZone: http://localhost:8761/eureka
And they include:


<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-netflix-eureka-client</artifactId>
</dependency>

🧪 Test It
Now you can call:


http://localhost:8080/users/all
http://localhost:8080/orders/list
➡ Gateway will:

Route the request

Look up the actual service from Eureka

Forward it to the correct instance

✅ Summary
| Step              | Description                                    |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Add dependencies  | Spring Cloud Gateway + Eureka Client           |
| Enable Discovery  | Use `discovery.locator.enabled: true`          |
| Define routes     | Use `uri: lb://service-name`                   |
| Register services | Make sure all microservices are Eureka clients |
